<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Eight Princes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">291–<a href="/years/306">306</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Rebellion of the Eight Kings</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Eight Princes was a series of civil wars among princes of the Sima family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-jin-dynasty">Western Jin dynasty</a>, fought from 291 to 306 for control of the mentally incapable Emperor Hui. Triggered by Empress Jia's intrigues, it devastated northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and exhausted the dynasty, enabling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and other peoples, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yuan">Liu Yuan</a> of Han-Zhao, to rise against Jin.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>War of the Eight Princes</b> (291–306) wrecked the Western Jin, the Xiongnu-led Han Zhao state sacked Luoyang in 311, capturing Emperor Huai, and took Chang'an in 316, capturing Emperor Min.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sui reunified China proper, closing the Northern and Southern dynasties era and the division that had lasted since the <b>War of the Eight Princes</b>, and in a short reign laid much of the groundwork for the Tang, which toppled them and then presided over a golden age.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Liu Yuan, a descendant of the Southern Xiongnu shanyus educated in the Chinese classics, rebelled during the <b>War of the Eight Princes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Emperor Hui, who was mentally incapable, Empress Jia's intrigues triggered the <b>War of the Eight Princes</b> from 291 to 306.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-jin">Western Jin</a></li></ul></section>
